=== vocabulary ===
An alert is a kind of signal.
A service is a kind of system.
An incident is a kind of process.
A deploy is a kind of event.
A runbook is a kind of artifact.
An oncall engineer is a kind of role.
An SLO is a kind of metric.
A postmortem is a kind of artifact.
A status update is a kind of event.

An alert has a name, a severity, a status, a service name, and a fired at.
An alert can be firing or acknowledged or resolved, called the lifecycle.
A service has a name, a status, an owner, and an open incident count.
An incident has a number, a severity, a status, a service name, and an opened at.
A deploy has a version, a status, and a deployed at.
A runbook has a name, a service name, and a url.
An oncall engineer has a name and a region.
An SLO has a name, a target, and a current value.
A postmortem has an incident number, a status, and a summary.
A status update has a message and a posted at.

A service is keyed by the name.

Definition: an alert is critical if its severity is "critical".
Definition: an alert is stale if its fired at is within the last 15 minutes.
Definition: a service is degraded if its status is "degraded".
Definition: a deploy is unhealthy if its status is "failed".
Definition: an incident is high severity if its severity is "sev1" or its severity is "sev2".
Definition: an incident is open if its status is "open".
Definition: an SLO is breached if its current value is less than its target.
Definition: an alert is actionable if it is critical and its status is not "resolved".
Definition: a service is healthy if its status is "healthy".
Definition: a service is recovered if the deploy step has ever succeeded.

Firing relates a service to an alert.
Firing is read via the alert.list tool.
Ownership relates an oncall engineer to a service.
Ownership is read via the oncall.roster tool.
Coverage relates a runbook to a service.
Coverage is read via the runbook.lookup tool.

The verb to page (it pages, it is paged) means the ownership relation.
The verb to own (it owns, it is owned) means the ownership relation.
The verb to cover (it covers, it is covered) means the coverage relation.

To fetch firing alerts:
  bind alerts = invoke alert.list with status = "firing".

To fetch open incidents:
  bind incidents = invoke incident.list with status = "open".

To fetch services:
  bind services = invoke service.list.

To fetch runbooks:
  bind runbooks = invoke runbook.list.

To fetch unhealthy deploys:
  bind deploys = invoke deploy.list with status = "failed".

To page the oncall engineer for an alert:
  invoke page.send with alert = the alert.

To acknowledge an alert:
  invoke page.ack with alert = the alert.

To roll back a deploy:
  invoke deploy.rollback with deploy = the deploy.

To check the health of a service:
  invoke service.healthcheck with service = the service.

To open an incident for an alert:
  bind incident = invoke incident.open with alert = the alert.

To close an incident:
  invoke incident.close with incident = the incident.

To escalate an incident:
  invoke incident.escalate with incident = the incident.

To post a status update for an incident:
  bind status update = invoke status.post with incident = the incident.

To look up the runbook for a service:
  bind runbook = invoke runbook.lookup with service = the service.

To publish a postmortem for an incident:
  bind postmortem = invoke postmortem.publish with incident = the incident.

=== constants ===

The slo error budget threshold is 0.1.
The escalation cooldown is 10 minutes.
The maximum page attempts is 3.

=== instances ===

There is a service called pagerduty service:
  name = "pagerduty"
  status = "active"
  owner = "sre-platform"
  open incident count = 0

There is a service called statuspage service:
  name = "statuspage"
  status = "active"
  owner = "sre-platform"
  open incident count = 0

=== language ===
Comparison synonyms:
  breaches = is less than
  spikes above = is more than
  matches = is
Duration synonyms:
  oncall shift = 8 hours
Assertion synonyms:
  confirm
  guarantee that

=== trace ===
retention: 30 days
hash chaining: enabled
sample floor: 10
default monitor cadence: 1 days

=== tools ===
List Alerts
===========
-- List alerts by status.
~ alert.list(status: String) : List

List Incidents
==============
-- List incidents by status.
~ incident.list(status: String) : List

List Services
=============
-- List all services.
~ service.list() : List

List Runbooks
=============
-- List all runbooks.
~ runbook.list() : List

List Deploys
============
-- List deploys by status.
~ deploy.list(status: String) : List

Send Page
=========
-- Page the oncall engineer for an alert.
~ page.send(alert: Alert) : Boolean

Acknowledge Page
================
-- Acknowledge a page for an alert.
~ page.ack(alert: Alert) : Boolean

Rollback Deploy
===============
-- Roll back a deploy.
~ deploy.rollback(deploy: Deploy) : Boolean

Service Healthcheck
====================
-- Check the health of a service.
~ service.healthcheck(service: Service) : Boolean

Open Incident
=============
-- Open an incident for a firing alert.
~ incident.open(alert: Alert) : Incident

Close Incident
==============
-- Close a resolved incident.
~ incident.close(incident: Incident) : Boolean

Escalate Incident
=================
-- Escalate an open incident.
~ incident.escalate(incident: Incident) : Boolean

Post Status Update
===================
-- Post a status update for an incident.
~ status.post(incident: Incident) : StatusUpdate

Lookup Runbook
==============
-- Look up the runbook for a service.
~ runbook.lookup(service: Service) : Runbook

Publish Postmortem
==================
-- Publish a postmortem for a closed incident.
~ postmortem.publish(incident: Incident) : Postmortem

Oncall Roster
=============
-- Look up the oncall engineer covering a service.
~ oncall.roster(service: Service) : OncallEngineer
